    "reading": {
        "body": "Alabama's documented data center capacity stands at just 5 MW across 11 facilities, a figure that places it far outside the tier of states driving AI infrastructure growth.\n\nThe 11 facilities are not evenly distributed. Google operates three closely clustered sites in Jackson County, and Meta lists three entries in Madison County under the same name and operator, suggesting campus-style expansion at both locations rather than 11 independent deployments. The only facility with a confirmed power figure is DC Blox in Birmingham, at 5 MW, which accounts for all the megawatts the data records. The remaining ten facilities carry no capacity numbers, so the true aggregate load is unknown and almost certainly higher than 5 MW.\n\nFor AI workloads, which demand dense, sustained power, 5 MW of confirmed capacity is negligible. A single large GPU training cluster can exceed that figure alone. The presence of Google and Meta signals that hyperscaler interest in the state exists, but the data does not include build-out timelines, planned capacity, or grid interconnection status, so nothing here supports conclusions about Alabama's trajectory as an AI compute location.\n\nWhat the data covers is a snapshot of facility locations and operators as of mid-2026. What it does not cover is power availability, grid constraints, cooling infrastructure, or whether the state's utility base can support the load growth that hyperscaler campuses typically require.",
